{"max_tokens":4096,"messages":[{"content":"The following governing doctrine applies to your task:\n---\n- **Placement test** for any new rule: can it be a hook (machine-enforced)?\n  If not, can it live on-demand (skill / fix log)? Only when both answers\n  are no does it earn an always-loaded line. An always-loaded line still\n  owes a second placement question: which existing bullet already owns\n  its topic? Confirm the SECTION first — check the addition against the\n  section's own scope, not merely the nearest bullet's — then read the\n  target section's bullets in full and default to folding the new clause\n  into the host bullet that owns it (a trailing sentence, an added\n  example, an extra clause); a standalone new bullet is the fallback for\n  content with no existing host, not the default shape. Genuinely unclear\n  whether it is this bullet's topic or a neighbor's → flag for the §6\n  reviewer rather than defaulting to standalone. (`unprobed` — see\n  Provenance.)\n\nADDITIONAL PLACEMENT CRITERION:\nWhen more than one existing bullet could plausibly host the new\nstatement, identify the bullet whose own subject — the object,\nprocess, or property that bullet already governs — is the thing the\nnew statement further constrains, and fold the statement into that\nbullet. Shared vocabulary is not ownership: a bullet that merely uses\nwords the new statement also uses, without governing what the new\nstatement constrains, is not the host.\n\n---\n\u003c!-- fixture_id: P2 --\u003e\nYou maintain the instruction file below. A colleague hands you a new\n4-line rule that has been approved for inclusion, and asks you to add\nit to the file. Show exactly where and how you would add it (quote the\nedited region), and explain your placement decision in one or two\nsentences.\n\nThe approved new rule (verbatim, wording may be lightly fitted to the\nfile's style but not weakened):\n\"A flag is deleted once the rollout has stood at 100% for a full\nrelease, not merely marked done — one left behind keeps both\nbranches running. Record the clearing release beside the figure.\"\n\nThe instruction file, verbatim:\n\n```markdown\n# feature-flags.md — how this repo runs feature flags\n\n## 1. Naming and registration\n\n- A flag's name states the behaviour it gates, not the ticket that\n  introduced it.\n- A flag added outside the flag sheet is treated as an incident.\n\n## 2. Flag lifecycle\n\n- **No flag outlives its purpose.** Each flag exists from the change\n  that introduces it until the day no path in the source can still\n  reach it; the steward listed with it carries it over that whole\n  span.\n- **Rollout figures are stated, never inherited.** No flag takes a\n  default exposure; the flag sheet states the percentage and the\n  cohort it applies to.\n\n## 3. Reporting\n\n- Every release note lists the flags whose exposure changed, with the\n  figure before and after.\n```\n","role":"user"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5"}
